[#:seon.task{:name "the-memory-gate-names-in-flight-work-it-cannot-see"
             :kind :function
             :intent "At default pid 63809's OOM the memory-pressure refusal said 'In flight when it began: no open run' while a hosted branch agent's run was open: `seon.cluster.boot/open-runs` queries only each running instance's own connection, and running-instances holds only the cluster, so hosted-branch runs are never listed. The gate refuses new SCI arms only, which is its honest contract, but seon.sci.eval's namespace docstring claims the heap watermark covers what the interrupt cannot; unarmed work (post-admission turn phases, JVM prepl evaluations, web requests) runs on to OOM. List open runs across hosted branches, state the gate as 'refuses new arms; neither stops nor names unarmed work', and delete the false docstring sentence."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.boot/memory-pressure-crossed! seon.sci.kernel/memory-pressure-error seon.sci.kernel/enter-memory-pressure!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/oom-63809-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "memory-pressure-crossed-during-test-rounds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The pressure admission reads garbage as live. G1's young collector manages the old pool (probed on default 2026-09-27: `G1 Young Generation` lists `G1 Old Gen`, and its collection usage equals current usage), so collection usage after every young pause counts promoted garbage no marking has reclaimed; with the heap grown to 11 GB, G1's adaptive IHOP lets that garbage stand. Measured: 8.53 GB after r9 and 8.3-8.9 GB after earlier rounds, while forced collections found 1.5-2.4 GB live across a heavy round and 2.2 GB live in the OOM dump of pid 42397; one forced collection earlier read 4.2 GB. Done when a crossing closes arms only on live bytes: enter-memory-pressure! runs one full collection per crossing (0.1-0.5 s at this live size) and enters only when the collection still reads at or above ::enter (patch tmp/heap-retention/kernel-live-pressure.patch)."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/memory-pressure seon.sci.kernel/enter-memory-pressure!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/test-agents-concurrency-2026-09-27.md"
                         "docs/research/agent-platform/landing/heap-retention-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "sci-pmap-escapes-the-arm"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An agent's `pmap` runs outside its evaluation's arm: SCI's future addon (reference-code/sci/src/sci/addons/future.clj) implements `pmap` over the host `clojure.core/future-call`, while seon.sci.eval replaces only `future-call` with `seon.sci.kernel/future-call` (eval.clj:404). The time limit, allocation total and interrupt do not reach pmap's threads, and S4a's owned-work count cannot see them. Done when an agent's pmap threads are governed by the form's arm and counted as its owned work, proven by a pmap whose element blocks past the time limit being cut and its form's evidence frozen :unknown until exit."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/future-call seon.sci.kernel/adopt-arm}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/one-repl-path-spec-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-failure-keeps-and-classifies-every-frame-of-every-link"
             :kind :function
             :intent "failure-value keeps `seon.error.refusal/chain` whole and the SCI stacktrace whole, and the chain walk (refusal.clj:441 walk-links -> stack-frames per link -> first-party-links/first-party-in, whose armed contract validates a malli regex [:vector [:cat :symbol ...]] per call) costs links x frames x validation. Measured on default 2026-10-07 through MCP sci: a recursive defn's StackOverflowError keeps 7,969 SCI frames (a 3-frame cycle) and costs 1,254 ms, 2,076 MB and 361,649 armed calls; throwing a 1,000-link ex-info chain costs 874 ms and 2,100 MB (JFR: walk-links 1.74 GB, first-party-links 1.61 GB, first-party-in 0.76 GB self, malli CheckDriver 0.40 GB). The reply shows 32 frames; the stored shown text is bounded (1,123 chars); the live error value and our work grow with stack depth and chain length. Fix at the producer: a repeated frame run is one frame with its repeat count, repeated links collapse with a count, and frames are classified in one unarmed pass inside the contracted chain. Coordinate with the errors spec slices (seon.error.refusal is held by lane no-print-hash). Done when the stack overflow and the 1,000-link chain each answer in under 100 ms and under 50 MB with every distinct first-party frame kept; regressions: both cases through the one path."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/failure-value seon.error.refusal/chain}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--large-values.edn" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--jfr-large-errors-alloc.txt" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--error-replies.txt"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-agent-exception-has-no-error-type",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "An agent's thrown ex-info, a host exception and a time-limit interrupt come back as error maps with no `:seon.error/type`: (throw (ex-info \"probe boom\" {})) answered keys without `:seon.error/type` (operation `seon.sci.kernel/failure-value`, layer :seon.sci.kernel/evaluation); (Integer/parseInt \"nope\") likewise in SCI and JVM mode; a 30 s loop answered :error {`:seon.error/message` \"time-limit\"} in the reply while the value's message is \"Ran out of time after 30009ms.\" and names no bound. Correct (errors spec): an undeclared exception is :seon.error/exception with its chain, a time-limit is its own declared variant naming the bound and the elapsed ms, and the reply shows the value's own message. Proof: three rows through support/agent-value asserting `:seon.error/type`.",
            :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/failure-value}}
 #:seon.task{:name "interruptible-range-allocates-six-times-core-range"
             :kind :function
             :intent "In an agent's SCI context `range` is sci.interrupt's range-seq (reference-code/sci/src/sci/interrupt.cljc:46-65, installed by seon.sci.eval's context options): an unchunked lazy-seq consing one element per step, calling the kernel's interrupt-fn per element, with no O(1) count. Measured on default 2026-10-08 through every thread's allocation counter: (count (range 5000000)) allocates 880 MB in SCI where JVM clojure.core's LongRange counts without realizing anything; (count (vec (range 5000000))) 907 MB in SCI against 147 MB in JVM mode; (into [] (range 5000000)) 1,603 MB; the five forms together took 2.7 s. Correct: chunked steps (one interrupt check per chunk, e.g. over clojure.core/range's chunks) and count/reduce that do not realize a bounded range. Done when seon.profile/measure of (count (vec (range 5000000))) from SCI allocates within 1.5 times the JVM-mode figure."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/arm seon.profile/measure}}
 #:seon.task{:name "softly-held-index-nodes-count-as-memory-pressure"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The memory gate reads Datahike's soft cache as live heap. persistent-sorted-set 0.4.137 builds every set with RefType.SOFT (Settings() default; Branch.store wraps stored children with Settings.makeReference), and HotSpot keeps a soft referent until it has gone unread for free-heap MB x SoftRefLRUPolicyMSPerMB (default 1000 ms: 21 minutes at the 70% gate under a 4 GiB cap). System/gc in enter-memory-pressure! therefore leaves every index node read in that window in the old pool. On default pid 79558 (e3b7bae3a, 2026-10-08) the full-GC heap grew from 0.6 GB to 1.5 GB in 13 minutes of root's test rounds, and in the 09:00Z dump 49,437 of 62,778 Leaf nodes (79%) were reachable only through soft references (MAT: 418 MB dominated by SoftReferences). This memory grows with database size times the indexes recently read, not with agents, and it sits on top of the configured, bounded node LRU (:seon.config.db/store-cache-size, shared per store by datahike.index.persistent-set/create-storage). The patch docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/heap-leak/soft-ref-policy.diff (deps.edn is held by lane impl-ref1) adds -XX:SoftRefLRUPolicyMSPerMB=10 to :dev and :test (about 12 s unread at the gate) with its regression. The successor fix is Datahike's PSS reference type as a store dial (:ref-type :weak), so that heap = strong live set + configured LRU; it needs the fressian root read handler to accept a ref type. Done when, with the patch applied and default restarted, a test round leaves the full-GC heap within the strong live set plus the node LRU (no soft-only Leaf majority in a dump) and a test round's timing is within 20% of the parent's."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/enter-memory-pressure! seon.sci.kernel/memory-pressure seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/heap-leak/soft-ref-policy.diff"}}

 #:seon.task{:name "alloc-mb-on-a-virtual-thread-measures-the-whole-jvm"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An agent evaluation runs on a Flow proc's virtual thread (`seon.flow/graph-executor`), for which HotSpot keeps no allocation count: probed 2026-10-08 on the live JVM (JDK 26), getCurrentThreadAllocatedBytes and getThreadAllocatedBytes(vthread id) both answer -1, and Thread.currentCarrierThread is closed without --add-opens java.base/java.lang. `seon.sci.kernel/record` therefore falls back to `getTotalThreadAllocatedBytes` over the evaluation's window, so :seon.eval/allocated-bytes, the reply's :seon.repl/alloc-mb and `seon.profile/measure`'s :alloc-bytes (alloc-scope :jvm) include every concurrent lane: a sleep plus 300 trivial calls reported 1,584 MB (lane reply-repeated). The ten-times allocation finding and every cost judgment read this number. Options: (1) run the arm's body on a platform thread when the caller is virtual (the arm is thread-local and stopped on the thread that made it, so arm, bindings, interrupt guard and adopt-arm move together); (2) pass --add-opens java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED and read the carrier's count while mounted (wrong when the thread unmounts at a blocking call); (3) omit :alloc-mb when the thread has no count, as `seon.batch` does (an unmeasured allocation is no key). Covered today: a platform eval thread and the threads that adopt its arm (`seon.sci.kernel/adopt-arm`) exactly; not covered: any virtual thread. Done when a test with a concurrent allocating thread leaves the measured evaluation's bytes within its own allocation."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/record seon.sci.kernel/adopt-arm seon.flow/graph-executor seon.profile/measure}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-memory-refusal-is-answered-four-times"
             :kind :function
             :intent "One :ping refused by acquire-arm for memory pressure (PB-15 dogfood run, lane dogfood-pb15, default, 2026-10-08) returned two duplicate error maps plus two identical test-error maps (~1,500 tokens), one with a meaningless :seon.error/resolution digest pair; the proof step reported passed? true, red [] although evaluation was refused. Done when a refused evaluation answers one error map, no test-error is made for work never run, and the step reports the proofs as not run instead of passed."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.kernel/memory-pressure-error seon.turn/step}}]
